  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+\u0394x,0+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u2248\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u0394x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u0394y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}=f(0.2,0.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.2,0.1).$\n\nMoving from $(0,0)$ to $(0.2,0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =0.2-0=0.2, \\\\ \u0394y & =0.1-0=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u2248\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.15.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.2,0.1) & \u2248f(0,0)+0.15 \\\\ \\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1} & \u2248\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}+0.15 \\\\  & =1+0.15 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
